A for statement is probably one of the most basic ways of looping through code. A for statement lets you repeat a block of code multiple times, one after another.  For example, we can say:

for(i=0; i<10; i++)
{
     cout << "a";
}

In this case, a loop would run from i=0 to i=9, or until i reaches the value of 10.  This means that every time you run the loop, the output would be "a" (or many "a"s, if you had more than ten items).  
